  "notes": "Teen R&B group out of the Chris Stokes / MTM camp — youth-market new jack swing moving into hip-hop soul, 1994–2002. The research gives \"59–94; ballads 59–68 half-time, uptempos 82–94 straight\", and the uptempo band is the one encoded because it is the half whose kit the entry actually specifies: \"a swung 16th hat on the uptempos\" against \"a bare kick/snare-on-3 on the ballads\". So the base is 82–94 straight with `halfTime` false; the ballad band is a different backbeat class and is not folded in. The three drum deltas are the swung 16th hat itself (0.6 swing, continuous, fill density at 0.5), a backbeat with real ghost activity on the `e` slots, and a snap/shaker/tambourine bed the parent does not carry in that combination. Harmony is \"two- to four-chord vamps, Rhodes/synth-brass colour\", with the major families leading because the entry says \"major-leaning on the uptempos\". \"Three-part harmony in a high tenor range (the group's age is the timbral signature)\" is the high melody register with a narrow interval spread; \"low note-density with long held hook notes\" is the 2–6 density, the legato articulation and the empty-bar bias. ⚠ Confidence is **low**: the research rates the entry medium-low and marks the key claim `[LOW-CONF]`, so the keys here are lane-level rather than named. ⚠ Every velocity, density and register number, and `swing.amount` 0.60, is INFERRED.",
